WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.125 --> 00:00:03.000
Ever since I read the book “In
Praise of Shadows”,

00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:06.000
I've always had this image of Japan.

00:00:06.250 --> 00:00:07.083
But when I visited it

00:00:07.083 --> 00:00:11.375
for the first time in August,
it wasn't quite what I expected.

00:00:11.458 --> 00:00:15.750
Even the places that Jun’ichirō Tanizaki
beautifully describes in his book,

00:00:15.750 --> 00:00:19.666
it was almost impossible
to experience them as they were intended.

00:00:19.833 --> 00:00:24.791
As an architect trying to identify
my own philosophy on design, I felt myself

00:00:24.791 --> 00:00:28.750
desperately trying to cling to this idea
of this mysterious place.

00:00:28.833 --> 00:00:32.958
But every time I tried to reach it,
it kept getting away from me.

00:00:33.041 --> 00:00:35.083
Was I too late?

00:00:35.083 --> 00:00:39.375
Does my idea of Japan still exist?

00:00:39.458 --> 00:00:42.750
I decided to travel to a place
where space, sound

00:00:42.750 --> 00:00:46.291
and time are perceived
in a completely different way.

00:00:46.500 --> 00:00:51.250
A place where people embrace
and craft the shadows.

00:00:51.333 --> 00:00:55.791
Japan.

00:00:55.875 --> 00:01:04.833
a portion of this video
sponsored by Fiverr.

00:01:04.916 --> 00:01:06.875
This is the Sayama pond

00:01:06.875 --> 00:01:09.958
about an hour
and a half away from the heart of Osaka.

00:01:10.041 --> 00:01:13.041
And we came here hoping to experience
something different.

00:01:13.125 --> 00:01:17.500
It's Japan's oldest reservoir
dating back to the seventh century.

00:01:17.583 --> 00:01:20.916
And for generations, the water
from this pond nurtured the land

00:01:20.916 --> 00:01:22.458
and sustained the communities.

00:01:22.458 --> 00:01:25.791
In the Shintoist or animist traditions
that's rooted in Japanese

00:01:25.791 --> 00:01:29.708
culture, places like this
aren't just resources or landscapes.

00:01:29.791 --> 00:01:32.958
They're the dwelling places of the spirits
who protect the land.

00:01:33.083 --> 00:01:36.541
And you can feel this reverence
for nature at the Sayamaike Museum,

00:01:36.541 --> 00:01:40.250
which was designed by the Pritzker
Prize winning architect Tadao Ando.

00:01:40.416 --> 00:01:41.416
The museum covers

00:01:41.416 --> 00:01:44.875
the history of Japan's flood
control measures and irrigation systems.

00:01:44.875 --> 00:01:48.250
But it's a lot more than that.

00:01:48.333 --> 00:01:51.041
Standing there I felt small,

00:01:51.041 --> 00:01:56.625
like I was being swallowed
by the presence of something grand.

00:01:56.708 --> 00:01:59.875
Simple geometries
create a sense of visual harmony.

00:01:59.875 --> 00:02:03.375
And all of this made me pause.

00:02:03.458 --> 00:02:06.958
There's rituals twice
every hour for 5 minutes.

00:02:07.000 --> 00:02:09.208
There's a waterfall that you can walk
under.

00:02:09.208 --> 00:02:10.250
A little further down.

00:02:10.250 --> 00:02:13.875
There's a circular courtyard,
and walking up the length of the ramp,

00:02:13.958 --> 00:02:17.791
as you circle around the geometry,
it slows down your pace

00:02:17.791 --> 00:02:21.625
before entering the museum,
just like a medieval labyrinth,

00:02:21.666 --> 00:02:25.208
which purposely slows you down
by taking the long route.

00:02:25.375 --> 00:02:29.166
When you finally enter the museum,
you're faced with a monumental structure.

00:02:29.333 --> 00:02:31.791
It's a segment
from the old bank of the pond.

00:02:31.791 --> 00:02:35.125
And as you walk down this long ramp,
You can almost feel

00:02:35.125 --> 00:02:38.500
the history
embedded in each layer of the sediment.

00:02:38.666 --> 00:02:40.625
This determines
the height of the building.

00:02:40.625 --> 00:02:41.583
Meaning the building

00:02:41.583 --> 00:02:46.041
is the exact depth of the pond,
creating the symbolic connection.

00:02:46.291 --> 00:02:50.750
Then there's the natural light,
which is celebrated and made more

00:02:50.750 --> 00:02:57.791
beautiful by putting it against concrete
and creating a deep shadow around it.

00:02:57.875 --> 00:03:01.875
When Jun’ichirō Tanizaki describes
the shadows in traditional

00:03:01.875 --> 00:03:06.541
Japanese esthetics, he's
talking about this:

00:03:06.625 --> 00:03:09.708
The layered glow of the shoji screen,
the subtle

00:03:09.708 --> 00:03:14.875
soft daylight that enters through the deep
eaves of a Japanese roof.

00:03:14.958 --> 00:03:17.333
But Tadao Ando, he takes this

00:03:17.333 --> 00:03:25.541
into a whole new dimension.

00:03:25.625 --> 00:03:26.291
Tadao Ando

00:03:26.291 --> 00:03:31.083
was actually a self-trained architect,
and in 1964, when he was just 24 years

00:03:31.083 --> 00:03:36.000
old, he traveled to Rome and visited
the Pantheon.

00:03:36.083 --> 00:03:39.125
There
he found a very different kind of light

00:03:39.125 --> 00:03:41.250
than the one that's praised in his home
country.

00:03:41.250 --> 00:03:45.625
The single central cut out in the Pantheon
created this dramatic gesture.

00:03:45.750 --> 00:03:46.708
It's stark.

00:03:46.708 --> 00:03:50.083
And in certain moments,
it cuts through the space like a knife.

00:03:50.291 --> 00:03:57.833
Apparently, this was the moment
he decided to become an architect.

00:03:57.916 --> 00:03:59.041
Throughout our trip,

00:03:59.041 --> 00:04:02.041
we visited a lot of Tadao
Ando's buildings.

00:04:02.208 --> 00:04:06.750
And I think what's really unique
about his architecture is how he merges

00:04:06.750 --> 00:04:11.458
the nuanced Japanese shadow with a bold,
almost theatrical kind of light.

00:04:11.625 --> 00:04:16.291
He said “the light becomes glorious only
when it's lit against the background

00:04:16.291 --> 00:04:21.750
of the deepest darkness.” In his spaces.,
even the gentlest, most restrained

00:04:21.791 --> 00:04:26.666
daylight becomes a spectacle, and it's
sculpts the concrete with a deep shadow.

00:04:26.833 --> 00:04:29.833
And for brief moments of the day,
a slit of light comes in, that breaks

00:04:29.833 --> 00:04:35.166
the concrete surface,
and intensifying the spatial depth.

00:04:35.250 --> 00:04:41.458
And in
these moments, time becomes tangible.

00:04:41.541 --> 00:04:43.416
But the more time I spent in Japan,

00:04:43.416 --> 00:04:46.458
I realized this obsession with shadows...

00:04:46.458 --> 00:04:52.250
...It's not just about what we see
or even understand with logic.

00:04:52.333 --> 00:04:55.041
(whispering)

00:04:55.041 --> 00:04:59.375
This is St Mary's Cathedral in Tokyo
by Kenzo Tange.

00:04:59.458 --> 00:05:01.458
It reminds me of something that was made

00:05:01.458 --> 00:05:05.250
not by humans, but crafted by the immense
force of nature.

00:05:05.416 --> 00:05:09.875
Sitting here I felt heavy and weightless
at the same time.

00:05:10.125 --> 00:05:12.416
There's a concept in Japanese esthetics.

00:05:12.416 --> 00:05:13.166
“Yūgen”.

00:05:13.166 --> 00:05:18.958
it comes from Zen Buddhism and speaks
to a profound, mysterious sense of beauty

00:05:19.000 --> 00:05:20.041
of the universe...

00:05:20.041 --> 00:05:23.625
...and
also the sad beauty of human suffering.

00:05:23.708 --> 00:05:25.208
At St Mary's Cathedral

00:05:25.208 --> 00:05:28.958
there's an atmosphere
that's both awe-inspiring and enigmatic.

00:05:29.125 --> 00:05:32.625
It really does
give you the sense of an unknowable beauty

00:05:32.625 --> 00:05:38.000
that's profound but remains just beyond
the grasp of full comprehension.

00:05:38.083 --> 00:05:42.833
But actually this “feeling” might
just be a deliberate design choice

00:05:42.833 --> 00:05:47.250
by Kenzo Tange, trying to avoid
any obvious symbolism at the cathedral.

00:05:47.458 --> 00:05:49.875
If you look at the building
from an aerial view,

00:05:49.875 --> 00:05:53.083
you will see that
the window openings form a cross.

00:05:53.166 --> 00:05:58.166
But unless you can a fly, most of us will
never see the building from this angle.

00:05:58.291 --> 00:06:01.458
You can only feel the presence
of the cross

00:06:01.541 --> 00:06:04.375
as two lines of light
illuminating the space.

00:06:04.375 --> 00:06:07.583
He also blurs
the line between walls and ceiling,

00:06:07.583 --> 00:06:12.000
creating a single continuous form
that challenge our sense of boundaries.

00:06:12.083 --> 00:06:15.500
This also creates
a very strong perspective and depth,

00:06:15.500 --> 00:06:18.541
making the Cathedral seem grander
than it really is.

00:06:18.750 --> 00:06:22.333
The textures add to this illusion
and it reminds you of something that

00:06:22.333 --> 00:06:25.708
you would find in nature.

00:06:25.791 --> 00:06:26.625
What was I saying?

00:06:26.625 --> 00:06:30.458
日本の建築は本当に素晴ら しかったです。

00:06:30.541 --> 00:06:32.375
What?

00:06:32.375 --> 00:06:33.833
are you okay?

00:06:33.833 --> 00:06:37.250
え、私、日本語を話しているの？
Ahh,

