00:07:36.958 --> 00:07:37.833
Zenbo Seinei

00:07:37.833 --> 00:07:41.750
is a meditation retreat in Awaji island
designed by Shigeru Ban.

00:07:42.000 --> 00:07:46.375
And here I experience some of these ideas
in a totally different light.

00:07:46.541 --> 00:07:49.416
It's a 100 meter long timber structure
built

00:07:49.416 --> 00:07:53.208
with a combination of pine, cedar
and other timbers from Japan.

00:07:53.333 --> 00:07:57.250
In fact, the whole place had this
lingering scent of Japanese cedar.

00:07:57.458 --> 00:08:00.208
It's respectfully integrated
into the landscape,

00:08:00.208 --> 00:08:03.375
aligning with a mountain ridge
rather than sitting above it.

00:08:03.375 --> 00:08:07.166
And almost hovering over the forest,
minimizing its footprint.

00:08:07.250 --> 00:08:11.333
And it creates this
delicate balance of nature and manmade.

00:08:11.458 --> 00:08:14.125
This was my very first meditation retreat,

00:08:14.125 --> 00:08:18.000
but I have practiced meditation
on and off for a lot of years.

00:08:18.000 --> 00:08:21.708
But this was a whole new experience
from my practice at home.

00:08:21.875 --> 00:08:24.208
It wasn't just about clearing the mind.

00:08:24.208 --> 00:08:33.208
It was about deepening the sensory
experience and my connection to nature.

00:08:33.291 --> 00:08:35.458
Zen Buddhism is about a direct

00:08:35.458 --> 00:08:40.125
experiential understanding
through meditation and mindfulness.

00:08:40.125 --> 00:08:41.291
And being present.

00:08:41.291 --> 00:08:45.166
it's about a connection with the ordinary
parts of life and with nature.

00:08:45.291 --> 00:08:48.458
So I think a lot of Japanese
designs are simple

00:08:48.458 --> 00:08:51.500
and understated,
but it highlights the things around us.

00:08:51.583 --> 00:08:55.833
In Tadao Ando’s spaces,
he highlights the trees or the sky

00:08:55.833 --> 00:09:00.208
or the people by framing them
and turning them into artworks.

00:09:00.375 --> 00:09:04.583
Here we highlight nature
using a simple flat roof

00:09:04.833 --> 00:09:09.666
and creating this steady, repetitive
rhythm with wood beams and columns.

00:09:09.833 --> 00:09:14.708
The central platform steps
up, stepping back from the glass railing.

00:09:14.833 --> 00:09:18.458
So when you're sitting on the platform,
you get an almost unobstructed view

00:09:18.458 --> 00:09:19.458
of the horizon.

00:09:19.458 --> 00:09:20.375
Sitting here...

00:09:20.375 --> 00:09:22.916
...It's kind of a sensory delight,

00:09:22.916 --> 00:09:26.583
and it made the meditation practice
almost effortless.

00:09:26.708 --> 00:09:29.666
I could feel the breeze,
the rustling of the leaves,

00:09:29.666 --> 00:09:37.750
the subtle scent of the forest,
the rhythmic humming of the cicadas.

00:09:37.833 --> 00:09:38.833
something that I noticed

00:09:38.833 --> 00:09:42.666
was how Japanese
people were so light on their feet.

00:09:42.750 --> 00:09:45.750
Even the guys, they walked like this,

00:09:45.916 --> 00:09:48.458
landing on the balls of their feet.

00:09:48.458 --> 00:09:52.541
Because in a wood building like this,
and also probably in traditional

00:09:52.541 --> 00:09:56.958
Japanese buildings,
if you're walking with the heels first,

00:09:57.041 --> 00:10:04.333
you can hear that sound
in the entire space...

00:10:04.416 --> 00:10:07.416
...and you feel extremely intrusive.

00:10:07.625 --> 00:10:09.958
And this was actually
a bit of a challenge for me,

00:10:09.958 --> 00:10:11.541
which is funny because my favorite

00:10:11.541 --> 00:10:15.291
part of the whole experience
was actually the walking meditation.

00:10:15.458 --> 00:10:20.333
As I focus on just walking,
I was surprised to feel the texture

00:10:20.333 --> 00:10:23.791
of the wood on my feet
and the subtle temperature differences

00:10:23.958 --> 00:10:26.958
in the part of the floor
that were shaded by the columns.

00:10:26.958 --> 00:10:30.208
But it's not just the meditation,
the whole building

00:10:30.250 --> 00:10:33.541
is essentially one long linear procession.

00:10:33.708 --> 00:10:37.625
And as you walk down the long halls
or the meditation platform,

00:10:37.625 --> 00:10:43.166
the rhythm of the structure helps
you slow down your pace.

00:10:43.250 --> 00:10:46.041
There's a Japanese concept, Ma.

00:10:46.041 --> 00:10:50.750
It's the space between elements, the time
between the beginning and the end.

00:10:50.916 --> 00:10:53.708
It's the gap, the space, or the pause.

00:10:53.708 --> 00:10:56.666
But Ma is not simply an absence,

00:10:56.666 --> 00:10:59.666
but an active presence
that shapes our experiences.

00:10:59.750 --> 00:11:03.875
And this concept captures
my experience here at Zenbo Seinei.

00:11:03.958 --> 00:11:07.125
Although I came here to pause,
my experience

00:11:07.125 --> 00:11:13.083
was actually quite full.

00:11:13.166 --> 00:11:15.791
We’ve visited
a lot of different places in Japan.

00:11:15.791 --> 00:11:18.500
But there was one place
that was a testament

00:11:18.500 --> 00:11:20.333
to some of these concepts
we're talking about.

00:11:20.333 --> 00:11:23.375
But the trip to this building
was not easy.

00:11:23.625 --> 00:11:27.166
It was about three and a half hours
transit from Awaji Island

00:11:27.166 --> 00:11:31.208
to this small port town
and another ferry to the island.

00:11:31.416 --> 00:11:33.875
All of this for this one building.

00:11:33.875 --> 00:11:35.416
It started raining on our ferry

00:11:35.416 --> 00:11:38.166
and it continued to rain
as we made our way up the hill.

00:11:38.166 --> 00:11:43.041
Walking up, we could see the beautiful,
lush landscape, and a change in the air.

00:11:43.125 --> 00:11:47.500
once we reached the site, there was this
winding path through different landscapes

00:11:47.500 --> 00:11:51.375
and soundscapes with a cool ocean breeze
coming from the left.

00:11:51.458 --> 00:11:55.375
At the end of the path is a large,
monolithic concrete dome

00:11:55.375 --> 00:11:59.541
sitting in the landscape.

00:11:59.625 --> 00:12:00.208
And when I

00:12:00.208 --> 00:12:04.083
entered,
the first thing I noticed is the silence.

00:12:04.166 --> 00:12:08.458
And then a strange sound
echoing in the space.

00:12:08.541 --> 00:12:11.541
Then you see that
everyone is kind of looking

00:12:11.541 --> 00:12:15.250
at the floor, either
standing around or sitting down.

00:12:15.375 --> 00:12:18.833
At first I thought the pool of water on
the ground was from the rain.

00:12:19.000 --> 00:12:19.625
And some of it

00:12:19.625 --> 00:12:23.416
definitely was because there were
two large openings open to the air.

00:12:23.583 --> 00:12:28.333
but when I look closer, I notice that
the small droplets of water were moving,

00:12:28.458 --> 00:12:33.583
trickling down ever so slowly,
combining with other droplets

00:12:33.750 --> 00:12:37.708
into these tiny holes in the concrete,
making a sound

00:12:37.958 --> 00:12:41.541
like this.

00:12:41.625 --> 00:12:42.791
I, along

00:12:42.791 --> 00:12:46.291
with dozens of other people,
sat there for hours.

00:12:46.375 --> 00:12:50.083
And slowly
you start to notice more and more details.

00:12:50.208 --> 00:12:53.625
I noticed that the space seemed to capture
sound even from a distance.

00:12:53.708 --> 00:12:58.875
For example, I noticed that a crow
flew overhead making this *crow sound

00:12:58.958 --> 00:13:01.958
but you could hear the sound echo
inside the dome.

00:13:02.041 --> 00:13:04.958
There were two large openings,
one facing the forest and one

00:13:04.958 --> 00:13:08.041
facing the sky,
which allowed the wind to funnel through.

00:13:08.166 --> 00:13:12.041
When the wind blew, the leaves
would rustle I could feel the wind

00:13:12.041 --> 00:13:16.166
brushing against me and a piece of string
which were hung on either holes

00:13:16.250 --> 00:13:21.583
would float ever so gently in the wind.

00:13:30.791 --> 00:13:31.000
This might sound like
an oversimplification,

00:13:31.000 --> 00:13:34.750
but there is a fundamental difference
between Western philosophy and Eastern

00:13:34.750 --> 00:13:39.291
philosophy, Western culture is rooted
in the enlightenment's ideals of reason

00:13:39.291 --> 00:13:44.041
and clarity, and light has become
a symbol of knowledge and progress.

00:13:44.291 --> 00:13:46.583
So, don't like shadows.

00:13:46.583 --> 00:13:49.416
We try to get as much light as possible.

00:13:49.416 --> 00:13:53.625
But Eastern culture is rooted in Shinto
and Buddhist philosophies,

00:13:53.833 --> 00:13:56.833
where they appreciate the ephemeral
and the subtle.

00:13:57.000 --> 00:14:02.125
And more than any other eastern country,
I think Japan has been able to preserve

00:14:02.125 --> 00:14:05.125
and refine this into their design
philosophy,

00:14:05.125 --> 00:14:09.458
which is why when you visit
a lot of their cities, you'll see this

00:14:09.583 --> 00:14:13.750
beautiful, seamless connection
between the old and the new.

00:14:13.833 --> 00:14:16.791
The problem is in today's visual culture

00:14:16.791 --> 00:14:20.416
driven by the algorithm,
there's not a lot of room for nuance.

00:14:20.625 --> 00:14:23.208
We want the quick reward.
We want the money shots.

00:14:23.208 --> 00:14:26.666
We want the maximum impact
in the shortest amount of time possible.

00:14:26.750 --> 00:14:31.625
And I guess that's
also kind of the irony of this video.

00:14:31.708 --> 00:14:36.708
I'm describing verbally and visually
what you should be experiencing

00:14:36.708 --> 00:14:40.166
with your whole body
over an extended period of time.

00:14:40.250 --> 00:14:42.958
So, I would still highly recommend

00:14:42.958 --> 00:14:45.416
you check out some of these places
for yourself.

00:14:45.416 --> 00:14:49.291
If you want the full travel
itinerary of the buildings that we visited

00:14:49.291 --> 00:14:51.500
and how we got there, we have created

00:14:51.500 --> 00:14:55.291
a PDF that you can download
and I'll link it in the description.

00:14:55.416 --> 00:14:56.875
my challenge to you...

00:14:56.875 --> 00:15:00.791
...describe a place that's stuck
in your mind for whatever reason.

00:15:00.958 --> 00:15:03.250
Try to be as detailed as possible.

00:15:03.250 --> 00:15:05.041
What did you see? What did you hear?

00:15:05.041 --> 00:15:06.166
What did you feel?

00:15:06.166 --> 00:15:09.083
You can leave your
responses in the comments.

